        723
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "7G–H Herodotus: Histories 4.110–116, the Amazons",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 5,
      "bbox": [
        263,
        191,
        314,
        909
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Now we change to Herodotus and the narrative style. You will often find that the sentences are longer, but the syntax will usually follow the sequence of events, with a series of participles leading on to the final event in the main verb.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 1,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 6,
      "bbox": [
        318,
        191,
        353,
        909
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Aorist participles are frequent, for where in English we would write ‘they attacked them and fought them’, the Greeks preferred to have only one main verb and to say ‘having attacked them, they fought them’.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 2,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 7,
      "bbox": [
        378,
        191,
        430,
        909
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "The second aorist participle has the same endings as the present participle, but has the aorist stem, GE pp. 151–152, #169–171: e.g. λαμβάνων present participle, λαβών aorist participle.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": 3,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
